Lift upstream's 32-bit iOS refusal at its eight sites (v-int-swift10).

Upstream guards each site with `#if os(iOS) && (arch(arm) || arch(i386))` and `fatalError("Unsupported
architecture")` in the #if branch; its #else body is plain Swift. This port exists to run on 32-bit iOS, so the
refusal branch is removed at each site and upstream's #else body is kept unchanged (the #if, the fatalError
line, the #else line and the matching #endif are removed; nothing else changes).

Sites (file, function, line in the tree after the lower-the-ios-availability patch):
  Sources/Crypto/Message Authentication Codes/HMAC/HMAC.swift  HMAC.init(key:)                          :65
  Sources/Crypto/Key Agreement/DH.swift                        SharedSecret hkdfDerivedSymmetricKey(using:...) :109
  Sources/Crypto/Signatures/ECDSA.swift                        P256.Signing.ECDSASignature init(derRepresentation:)  :86
  Sources/Crypto/Signatures/ECDSA.swift                        P256.Signing.ECDSASignature derRepresentation        :126
  Sources/Crypto/Signatures/ECDSA.swift                        P384.Signing.ECDSASignature init(derRepresentation:)  :247
  Sources/Crypto/Signatures/ECDSA.swift                        P384.Signing.ECDSASignature derRepresentation        :287
  Sources/Crypto/Signatures/ECDSA.swift                        P521.Signing.ECDSASignature init(derRepresentation:)  :408
  Sources/Crypto/Signatures/ECDSA.swift                        P521.Signing.ECDSASignature derRepresentation        :448

Size dependence of the #else bodies (read before the patch): HMAC.init uses SecureBytes capacity, freeCapacity and
byte loops, no pointer-size cast; SharedSecret uses HKDF over the shared secret, no size; the six ECDSA bodies use
coordinateByteCount (32, 48, 66) as Int counts and the ASN1 parser and serializer. The ASN1 length code
(ASN1.swift, a length shift on Int) is not in any refused body; the DER rows are measured on the 6.1.3 device.
